## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of practical, low-level timing data structures for efficiently managing many timers with bounded latency and predictable jitter, contrasting with heap-based approaches.

## Key Concepts
- Fixed 2048-bucket wheel with modulo mapping for O(1) timer insertion
- 1 ms granularity from 1 kHz tick thread providing time base
- Jitter characteristics: bounded to ±1ms, predictable execution patterns
- Locking strategies: per-bucket spinlocks for reduced contention vs global mutex for simplicity
- Cache locality benefits of bucket organization vs random heap access
- Timer lifecycle management: insertion, expiry handling, cancellation support
- High-load testing methodology with concurrency and stress scenarios

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo implementation sketch
#define BUCKETS 2048
typedef struct Timer {
    void (*cb)(void*);
    void* arg;
    uint32_t expiry;
    struct Timer* next;
} Timer;

static Timer* buckets[BUCKETS];
static pthread_mutex_t locks[BUCKETS];
static volatile uint32_t current_tick = 0;

void add_timer(void (*cb)(void*), void* arg, uint32_t delay_ms) {
    Timer* t = malloc(sizeof(Timer));
    t->cb = cb;
    t->arg = arg;
    t->expiry = current_tick + delay_ms;

    uint32_t bucket_idx = t->expiry % BUCKETS;
    pthread_mutex_lock(&locks[bucket_idx]);
    t->next = buckets[bucket_idx];
    buckets[bucket_idx] = t;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&locks[bucket_idx]);
}

void tick() {
    uint32_t bucket_idx = current_tick % BUCKETS;
    pthread_mutex_lock(&locks[bucket_idx]);
    Timer* t = buckets[bucket_idx];
    buckets[bucket_idx] = NULL;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&locks[bucket_idx]);

    while (t) {
        Timer* next = t->next;
        if (t->expiry <= current_tick) {
            t->cb(t->arg);
            free(t);
        }
        t = next;
    }
    current_tick++;
}
```

## Locking Strategy
**Per-bucket locking** reduces contention by allowing concurrent access to different buckets, improving scalability under high timer load. **Global locking** is simpler but becomes a bottleneck with many threads adding timers. Per-bucket spinlocks work well given minimal critical sections and good cache locality.

## Test Plan
1. **Functional correctness**: Schedule timers with varying delays, verify exact callback execution times
2. **Jitter analysis**: Measure actual vs expected execution times to validate ±1ms bounds
3. **Concurrency stress**: Multiple threads adding timers while tick thread processes
4. **Load testing**: Schedule thousands of concurrent timers to validate O(1) performance
5. **Edge cases**: Test with delay = 0, maximum delay, and rapid timer cancellation
6. **Memory validation**: Ensure no timer leaks under high scheduling/cancellation rates

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of crash-consistent data structures, persistent memory programming, and low-latency recovery mechanisms in real-world systems, connecting hardware flush instructions to correct ordering guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- Non-volatile memory semantics and persistence instructions (clwb, sfence)
- Write-ahead logging protocols and commit/rollback mechanisms for crash safety
- Epoch-based reclamation and idempotent recovery procedures
- Log replay and in-memory index reconstruction after power loss
- NUMA-aware memory layout and efficient log space management

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal sketch of log append and commit protocol
typedef struct {
    uint64_t key;
    uint64_t val;
    uint64_t epoch;
    uint8_t committed; // 0=pending, 1=committed
} log_entry_t;

// Put operation with crash consistency
int kv_put(uint64_t key, uint64_t val) {
    log_entry_t *entry = alloc_log_entry();
    entry->key = key;
    entry->val = val;
    entry->epoch = get_current_epoch();
    entry->committed = 0; // pending

    _mm_clwb(entry); // flush to NVRAM
    _mm_sfence();    // ensure ordering

    entry->committed = 1; // mark committed
    _mm_clwb(&entry->committed);
    _mm_sfence();

    update_index(key, val); // update in-memory index
    return 0;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of low‑level memory management, freelists, and fixed-size allocators.

## Key Concepts
- Fixed-size blocks and simple freelist
- In-block metadata for next pointer
- Alignment guarantees and fragmentation considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
// JavaScript-simulated fixed-size allocator on an ArrayBuffer
const POOL_SIZE = 64 * 1024;
const BLOCK_SIZE = 64;
const pool = new ArrayBuffer(POOL_SIZE);
const nextFree = new Int32Array(pool);
let head = 0;
function init_pool(){ const blocks = POOL_SIZE / BLOCK_SIZE; for(let i=0;i<blocks-1;i++){ nextFree[i] = i+1; } nextFree[blocks-1] = -1; head = 0; }
function my_malloc(){ if(head === -1) return null; const idx = head; head = nextFree[idx]; return new DataView(pool, idx*BLOCK_SIZE, BLOCK_SIZE); }
function my_free(ptr){ // ptr is DataView; compute index and push back to head
  const idx = Math.floor(ptr.byteOffset / BLOCK_SIZE);
  nextFree[idx] = head; head = idx;
}
```

